### 5.2 What each check proves

| Check | Property |
| --- | --- |
| `TestDPMatchesExhaustive` | For every exact output order, DP cost == cheapest unpruned bushy plan. |
| `TestRandomDPMatchesExhaustive` | Same equality on 15 random connected 3–5 relation instances. |
| `TestVerifyWithTruncatedOrders` | `MaxOrderLen=1` still optimal — truncation is lossless for the answer. |
| `TestMergeProducesReusableOrder` | Merge emits a reusable canonical key order; hash does not. |
| `TestHashRequiresEquiKey` | No hash/merge for Cartesian products. |
| `TestGreedyFallback` | Past `DPMaxRelations` the greedy plan is complete and finite. |
| `TestPlanCoversAllRelationsOnce` | Each relation once; masks/rows/per-operator costs consistent. |
| `TestInterestingOrdersUniverse` | ISO universe starts empty, respects `MaxOrderLen`, no duplicate columns. |
| `VerifyOptimal` in the demo | End-to-end re-enumeration confirms the DP winner. |

### 5.3 Why optimality is sound

1. `allJoins` sets `Rows = card(a.Mask | b.Mask)` — a subset-only value.
2. Parent cost depends only on children's `Cost`, `Rows`, `OutOrder` and fixed `card`; sort cost depends on `Rows`/`OutOrder` only.
3. Hence for fixed `(mask, order)` a minimum-`Cost` plan is a best building block; keeping it is safe.
4. Every partition `mask = L ⊎ R` is enumerated (bushy), and all operator variants/orientations are generated.
5. `ExhaustivePlans` prunes nothing; DP equalling its minimum for every order proves pruning removed no optimum.

### 5.4 Complexity

- DP: `O(3^n · |orders|)`, `|orders| ≤ Σ_{k≤M} P(p,k)`.
- Space: `O(2^n · |orders|)`.
- `DPMaxRelations` (default 10) → greedy `O(n²)` beyond it.
- `MaxOrderLen` (default 3); truncation is safe because longer keys satisfy shorter prefixes.

